  "final_message": "I fixed the bug in `session_ttl.py` and `test_public.py` now passes (1/1).\n\n**The bug:** line 10 removed the timezone from `now` but left it on `created`. With timezone-aware inputs like `+00:00`, Python can't subtract one from the other, so every call raised a `TypeError`.\n\n**The fix:** `now` is now parsed with plain `datetime.fromisoformat(now_iso)`, the same way `created` is. Aware timestamps are compared correctly across different offsets, and two naive timestamps still work.\n\nExtra checks I ran:\n- 30 seconds elapsed with a 60-second TTL returns `False`.\n- `00:00+00:00` vs `01:01+01:00` (61 seconds apart in real time) returns `True`.\n- Two naive timestamps 60 seconds apart return `True`.\n\nMixing one naive and one aware timestamp still raises `TypeError`. The README doesn't say how that case should behave, so I left it alone. `format_expiry_message` is unchanged.\n\nThe formatter flags this file, as it did `queue_util.py` in t3. It's probably the long line 7, which was already there before my edit, so I didn't touch it.\n\nDONE: Fixed `session_ttl_expired` by no longer stripping the timezone from `now` alone, so timezone-aware timestamps compare correctly; `test_public.py` passes.",
